WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:06.240
COVID-19 has come through and completely decimated and put on pause the bar and restaurant industry.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.820
We got about 8 million people out of work, and bar and restaurant owners struggling to come up with new ways

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.540
to possibly stay in business.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:18.020
I'm Jacob Greer I work in the bar and hospitality business, I'm also a freelance writer,

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:23.880
I write for Reason as well as other public policy outlets.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.180
Talking to other people in the industry, talking to bartenders they want two things.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:31.700
One they want to go back to work, but they also want to stay safe,

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:38.860
and it's really hard to do both at the same time if the only option is for people to come into a bar or restaurant

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.640
and to gather inside.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:47.110
We're seeing it's just a recipe for spreading the virus so we don't want to end up in a cycle of reopening and

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.040
then reclosing constantly, which we're seeing right now.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.660
We'd like to find something that's more stable.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:59.220
Talking to people in my industry the number one item on their wish list is to be free to sell cocktails to go.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.520
They want to be able to package a drink up to go alongside their food or even by itself,

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.580
for a customer to take home and drink.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.780
The other quick ideas would be to expand outdoor spaces for drinking which could be through

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:15.820
closing sidewalks, putting out tables, even closing down entire streets and creating public social plazas,

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.920
or if you want to go all the way they're eliminating open container laws entirely, so it's no longer a crime

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.600
to have a drink outside.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.740
So in much of the world, especially in Asia and other parts of Europe,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.060
it's not against the law it's not a crime to be outside drinking,

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.360
and that was the case in the United States as well.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.740
For most of our history it was not a crime simply to drink in public it was a crime to be drunk in public,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.440
and that's a key distinction.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:47.540
In the 1970's there became this push to make the law less discretionary, more objective,

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:53.260
and the way they did that was to say the act of drinking itself is now the crime,

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.840
and that highly constrained where people could drink.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.380
When we think of open containers we think of party atmospheres like Las Vegas,

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.920
or Bourbon Street in New Orleans, but there's also a lot more relaxed places

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.560
that have varying degrees of open container permission,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.500
like say Hood River in Oregon or Sonoma in California, it doesn't have to be a party atmosphere,

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.957
especially if you don't force everyone into one narrow space.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.120
It could be just allowing people to have a drink in the park,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.980
to have a picnic to walk around their neighborhood with a drink in hand.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:30.080
Prohibition happened in 1920 and contrary to popular image of the fun speakeasy with great cocktails,

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.460
what actually happened was we destroyed our cocktail culture in the United States through that period,

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:43.220
and we didn't really recover until the early 2000s, up until now and all of this has been hugely hit by the shutdown.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.600
Right now I view these laws about allowing us to do cocktails to go and expanding outdoor spaces

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.120
as much about damage control as anything else,

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.680
and to prevent this from being completely erased and giving it some chance to survive.

